The David Pakman show subreddit is a place
where more than 14,000 of our viewers and

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:12.661
listeners are regularly participating in respectful
and substantive political discussions. You

00:00:12.661 --> 00:00:17.670
can find that at David pakman.com/reddit a
few posts from today that I want to call to

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.580
your attention, a user named the goat of goats.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.280
Nine nine nine says wrong with a public option.
Don't most countries with universal healthcare

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.050
also allow private insurance. So this is a
very reasonable question and there's a couple

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.819
of things that are being mixed here. Uh, if
you have universal healthcare, it doesn't

00:00:35.819 --> 00:00:40.809
necessarily follow that you make private insurance
illegal. In fact, I don't think that should

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:47.280
be a priority. Let private insurance exist.
If the, uh, nationalize the universal healthcare

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.909
system, medicare for all or whatever else
is good enough, people are going to prefer

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:56.699
it over private insurance and this will naturally
as the free marketers like to say it will

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:02.550
naturally do what needs to happen to private
insurance. A public option is something else

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:11.240
and studies have shown that public options
would only lower premiums by about seven to

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:17.990
8%, uh, which is marginal when it comes to
the total lack of affordability of health

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.860
insurance for so many people in this country.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.689
So I think it's important to distinguish that
a public option is something that can exist

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:29.640
without having any kind of universal healthcare
or in addition to, and it is a separate question

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.710
from whether private for profit insurance
should be allowed to exist. Even if something

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:38.650
like Medicare for all is put in place. I happen
to think the answer is yes. Let it exist.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.030
Don't go through the hassle of saying we are
going to make your business illegal things.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:46.659
We'll just sort themselves out. If Medicare
for all or whatever government program is

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:51.341
put in place is good enough and people will
decide what it is that they prefer. Another

00:01:51.341 --> 00:01:57.590
post from User Ryan Lynn Burgo, uh, about
New Mexico rolling out a plan for tuition

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.590
Free State College. I don't, we don't need
to analyze the proposal specifically, but

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:08.860
I think that on both single payer health care
and tuition and dealing with homelessness,

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.970
I am very much in favor of states piloting
programs on their own.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:19.040
You will often hear a talking point from the
right on all of these social program proposals,

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.150
healthcare, whatever the case is that it would
not work in the United States. It works in

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:29.250
small European countries because they are
small and sometimes they say that it works

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.720
because the countries are homogenous. Although
it doesn't make any sense with regard to a

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:38.140
lot of these programs. A story for a different
day, I think it would be great for states

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.890
to pilot some of these programs. There are
many u s states with higher populations and

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:47.720
larger land masses than many European countries
and they would serve as great proving grounds.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:52.200
Unfortunately, Vermont's single-payer attempt
did not end up happening. But I would love

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.930
to see New Mexico do a tuition free college.
I would love for any state to show the viability

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.870
of these programs and also the economic benefits
that they would bring with them.

